Prove the following: Considering a normal $n$-gon inscribed in a unit circle, if a new $n$-gon is constructed interior to the $n$-gon such that each of its vertices' $x$ and $y$ coordinates are average those of some vertex of the outer polygon, all of the points are distinct. Therefore the radius of the circle inscribed in the new $n$-gon is smaller than that of the original $n$-gon. Let $x_k$ and $y_k$ denote the $x$-coordinate and $y$-coordinate of the vertex whose $x$-coordinate is the average of $x_1$ and $x_2$. Since the vertex whose coordinate $x_2$ has has negative $y$-coordinate, $x_k$ and $y_k$ are smaller than $x_1$ and $y_1$, respectively. By symmetry, this is also true for each of the other vertices.
The $x_1$ and $y_1$, the largest radius of the inscribed circle in the original polygon, is the same as the $x_k$ and $y_k$ because they are the distances on the $x$- and $y$-axis that span the whole polygon. By definition, the radius of the inscribed circle in the new polygon is $r=\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{r_1^2+r_2^2}$, where $r_1$ and $r_2$ are the $x$- and $y$-distances that span the new polygon, respectively, and by the last paragraph, these are $x_k$ and $y_k$. Thus the radius is $\frac{1}{2}r=\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{x_k^2+y_k^2}=\frac{x_k^2+y_k^2}{4r}$. By the first paragraph, $0<x_k^2+y_k^2\le 2$, so then $\frac{1}{2}r\le 1$. \begin{align*}
\qquad \Box
\end{align*}

